\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton was already seated at a corner table in a leather wingback the colour of old money, his back to the mahogany panelling from tropical forests that no longer existed. He preferred to see who might be eavesdropping. He liked rooms like this: rooms where decisions happened cleanly, and the consequences happened somewhere else, to people with names he\'92d never need to learn.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry arrived to find Dunhilton with a red-tipped Churchill-sized cigar between thumb and middle finger, and a brandy balloon in his other hand containing indiscreet ounces of blasphemous Tasmanian Scotch whisky. He gestured to the seat opposite. Gerry sat, trying not to breathe through his nose. Dunhilton knew he hated smokers, which was half the point.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A waiter appeared. \'93Scotch for the Commissioner?\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s fine,\'94 Dunhilton answered for him. The waiter departed.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton took a long pull on his cigar, held the smoke, exhaled across the table. \'93Operation Parramatta,\'94 he said quietly and emphatically. \'93We can\'92t wait till November now.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry had been dreading this. \'93No, your Serene Corporateness,\'94 he said reluctantly, as his eyes watered from the smoke.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We need to act now. You need to act. Whatever you come up with, you will have my complete support.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry deflected the implied threat by consulting his tablet. As he cradled it in one hand with its bottom edge resting on the table, he tapped and swiped. \'93I\'92m looking at Grayling Corporation\'92s 
\f2\i Entertainment Lifestyle Vocationally Integrated Servant: User Manual
\f1\i0 .\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton seized his own tablet to catch up with Gerry\'92s thinking. \'93Yes, yes! What about it? Do ELVIS units have \'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m looking at the Warranty Agreement.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton kept his face arranged in something that could pass for comprehension. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Warranty conditions effectively set out performance boundaries of a product,\'94 Gerry explained.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, yes, of course,\'94 Dunhilton said, because there were only so many times a man could admit he didn\'92t understand something before it became a habit. He picked up his cigar again and took an impatient puff.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Proper performance of a product cannot be guaranteed if the conditions are violated. They kinda define the product\'92s weaknesses.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Weaknesses we can exploit!\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, CEO.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton poked at his tablet like he was gutting a trout. Wanting to get ahead, he ventured, \'93Ah yes, I see. We could fly over the Ghetto and dump bacon and coffee on it. Presumably that\'92ll incapacitate them.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I like your thinking, your Serene Corporateness, but I\'92m wondering if even the most rancid fat or the hardiest coffee grounds would survive the Layer.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Right. Keep thinking.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry perused and scrolled.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Could we get them to inhale rhinestones?\'94 Dunhilton wondered, then shook it off and kept reading. \'93How about drugs?\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I rather suspect they are managing that on their own, CEO.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton grunted a reluctant acknowledgment. Smoke drifted between them in lazy spirals.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry suppressed the urge to cough. \'93I think it comes down to religion, CEO.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you mad? We\'92re in this mess because they\'92re already worshipping Sarah Jacobson as the Holy Momma.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If there\'92s one thing history has taught us, it is that the next best thing after having no religion...\'94 Gerry paused to catch his breath.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, go on, Gerry,\'94 Dunhilton said, adding an edge of mischief to the blade of his encouragement.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93\'85 is having at least two religions.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you saying we give them more religion?\'94 Incredulous, Dunhilton blew smoke in Gerry\'92s face.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Something they could fight over.\'94 Gerry coughed.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Elvi do like a good fight.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Indeed, Your S\'97\'94 Gerry coughed harder. \'93CEO.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And religious wars can go on for centuries.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry nodded, still holding his lungs in.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Wars can be very lucrative,\'94 Dunhilton conceded. \'93But can we contain it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do we need to? We are, after all, above the Layer,\'94 Gerry said, feeling as though he was inhaling the Layer with every breath.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To Dunhilton that seemed unusually reckless for Gerry. Perhaps the smoke was making him light-headed. Dunhilton made a mental note to conduct more meetings with Gerry in a cigar lounge or near a tyre fire. \'93Still, if they fight amongst themselves...\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Which we know they have a propensity for.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Things will go back to normal.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry scrunched his nose. \'93Tourism won\'92t like it. He wants that Elvi theme park so bad.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tax the tourists,\'94 Dunhilton swore, echoing the words of many a political leader down through the ages. \'93But still, it\'92s risky.\'94 Dunhilton looked Gerry straight in the eye as he reached into his breast pocket and took out his Coin of Allegiance. \'93On the question of whether we use religion against the Elvi: Profits for yes, Losses for no.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry gave a minimal nod and tightened his lips.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton tossed the coin high in the air, almost as high as the ceiling. It spun and shimmered as it ascended and fell. It clanked on the table, scattered ash from the tray, wobbled and almost rolled off before settling.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It came up Profits.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Religion it is then,\'94 Dunhilton said with relaxed confidence now that the matter had been decided. \'93What have you got in mind, Gerry?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I rather think my Elvi Rights predecessor had the right toggle, but failed to notice 
\f2\i we 
\f1\i0 could use it against 
\f2\i them
\f1\i0 .\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Spit it out, Commissioner.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry savoured visions of what he would unleash upon the Elvi and answered, \'93Gospel music.\'94
